@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — five searches carried in the Papyrus of London and Leiden
#
# The first four were carried on 2026-09-18 while the work was open, the fifth
# on 2026-10-09 when it was opened again to discharge prima-materia#747, all
# over the whole translation of recto and verso, in the edition of Griffith and
# Thompson of 1904. The digitised text separates its words with runs of two and three
# spaces, so a plain search for a phrase returns nothing; every count below was
# taken over a whitespace-normalised copy with the hits rewritten back into the
# raw text by position, which is the method AGENTS.md records for damaged
# scans. Three of the first four came back empty, and those three are the return
# on the reading that a reader loading the graph could not otherwise have. The
# fifth is a count and not a term search, and it is the one the graph needed in
# order to say how much of this manual is examinable at all.
#

pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the demotic manual names any outcome that would count against an inquiry"@en, "Ob das demotische Handbuch irgendeinen Ausgang nennt, der gegen eine Befragung zählte"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage naming a result of a correctly prepared inquiry under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work: a term after which he is to stop, a number of addresses beyond which the god is not to be pressed, or a sign that nothing will come. This is the material from which a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed out of a transmission's own mouth, and the same question was put to the Clavicula at pmp:ClaviculaFailureConditionSearch." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the translation, over a whitespace-normalised copy, for: in vain, fruitless, of no effect, no effect, useless, unsuccessful, leave off, give over, give up, break off, desist (none of them occurs); fail (two hits, one inside an invocation of the speaker's own strength, one the phrase without fail); faileth (one, inside an invocation); cease (eight; the only two that touch an operation are at XVII/17, where the operator ceases from the inquiry because the boy has looked away, and the clause about the gods going away and the boy ceasing to see them); abandon (one, in the editors' introduction); stop (four, all of stopping a vessel's mouth or staunching blood); not succeed (one, the purity clause at XVII/26); delay, obduracy, obstinacy, slow to come, do not come, not come (thirteen between them, every one of which is answered by a further formula and none by an end)." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Nothing came back, and what came back instead is the finding. Every hit that touches an inquiry at all is an escalation or an assignment: the compulsion, the further cry, the name said nine times until they answer truthfully, and the two general assignments to purity and to the boy's fright. The one place where a session is broken off is broken off because of the medium and not because of the god. The manual takes up the empty session six times and does not once treat it as a result."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the ground of the first clause of pmc:TheStrongerAddress, and it is recorded rather than summarised for the reason the class exists: without a node saying which terms were put and what came back, that clause would rest on an impression formed while reading, and a reader who suspects the impression would have to read the manual again."@en ;
    skos:note "Limits. One translation, one editor, one optical transcription, and a stopping rule could be phrased without any of the twenty-three terms above — as an hour at which the operator departs, say. Against that, the nine datings collected at pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna were read continuously for exactly that possibility and none of them states a consequence, and the columns that carry the graded compulsions were read in full and not only searched."@en .

pmp:PdmProbatumQuidSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the manual says what its nine trials consisted in"@en, "Ob das Handbuch sagt, worin seine neun Erprobungen bestanden"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "At the six places where the manual marks a procedure as tested, tried or well tested, and at the two where it gives the number nine: who carried the trials, on what question, against what the answer was afterwards set, and what an unsuccessful trial would have looked like." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search for: tested, tried, proved, approved, tries, trial, times tried, nine times, true and tried. The six marks are at IV/23, V/3, VIII/12, XIV/31, XXIV/1 and XXIX/1; each was read at its place together with the recipe it stands over and the recipe following it. Read as well: the editors' note to VIII/12, which compares the phrase with XIV/31 and XXIX/1 and holds nine times tried to be proverbial." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Not one of the six marks carries anything behind it. The mark is attached to the recipe and never to an outcome; no question is named, no answer is checked, and no trial is described. In one of the two numbered cases the number is not even the compiler's own — it is what he reports the informant as having said. The claim of nine trials therefore stands in a manual that pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch shows has no way of counting an unsuccessful one."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding is entered as a search and not as a node because it is an absence in the work, and the node it bears on, pmc:PdmProbatumNovies, carries what the work does say. The distinction matters here more than usual: a reader who meets tested nine times in a translation will supply the missing half by himself unless something in the record says it is missing."@en .

pmp:PdmReworkingSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the compiler says what he changed in a procedure he received"@en, "Ob der Zusammensteller sagt, was er an einem übernommenen Verfahren geändert hat"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "An addition the compiler makes to a procedure he says he received from somebody else — a term, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure that the donor's version is said not to have had. That is the material of pm:Reworking, and a manual that names four donors and cites competing rolls is the likeliest place in this holding to find it." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Every place where the work names a source or a variant was read at its wording: gave to me (I/1 and XVIII/7), said to me (XIV/1), he tells it (VIII/12), another roll says, another book says, another volume saith, another says, as says another book, otherwise said. Searched additionally for: I added, I found, I corrected, I have proved, better than, stronger than, is better." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "The compiler names his donors and sets his variants beside one another, and nowhere says that he changed anything or why one reading is preferred. The two comparative judgments the work does make are about procedures and not about versions: that this vessel-inquiry of the lamp is better than the beginning, and that there is none better than the amulet of the sixteen-thread band. Neither names what the worse one lacked."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding bears on how much a chain of copies is worth. This manual is at the near end of the demotic and Greek handbook tradition and at the far end of the grimoire stream, and it hands on procedures from at least four named persons without recording a single alteration. pm:Reworking counts the stations at which somebody took an instruction, carried it out and wrote down what was not in the exemplar; on the evidence of this work, this station is not one of them."@en .

pmp:PdmBothSidesSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the manual anywhere states both sides of a criterion"@en, "Ob das Handbuch irgendwo beide Seiten eines Kriteriums nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage that fixes an outcome to a perceptible event and says what the event would be in each of the two cases — the form pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls describes, and in particular the form its second clause says does not occur, an appearance announced to the participant beforehand that carries the failing side as well as the succeeding one." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Every conditional clause of the translation was read at its place; searched additionally for: if he says, if it be that, if you find, if thou, then she will, will not, shall not come to pass, otherwise, on the contrary. Three hundred and more conditionals stand in the work and almost all of them are instructions, not readings." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex, pmc:PdmProbatioGraviditatis, pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Three places in a manual of sixty-two columns, and their distribution is the finding. The pregnancy test and the assay of the stone are in the medical and pharmacological half of the verso, where nothing is being invoked; the third, the priest's inquiry at VIII/12-16, is in the divinatory half and is the only one there. Against those three stand the seven or eight announced signs of arrival at pmc:PdmSignaAdventus, none of which has a failing side, and the six escalations at pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, which make one impossible."@en ;
    skos:note "The one in the divinatory half is the reason this search was worth carrying. pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls asserts that where a criterion is an appearance announced to the participants beforehand, the transmission states the success side alone; here two distinct appearances are announced in advance and each is bound to one of the two answers before anything is seen. That is opposition to the second clause and is recorded at the protocol on it, pmp:BothSidesOfTheCriterionExamination, where a claim is corrected."@en .

pmp:PdmPraescriptaEnumerataSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "How many prescriptions this manual carries outside its divinatory half, and how many of them name a sign or a condition of failure"@en, "Wie viele Vorschriften dieses Handbuch ausserhalb seines divinatorischen Teils fuehrt und wie viele von ihnen ein Zeichen oder eine Misslingensbedingung nennen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns IV, XI to XVI, XIX to XXI, XXIII to XXVI and verso columns II to XXXIII, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel and Co., 1904. The divinatory columns were excluded because their signs and escalations were counted in the search of 2026-09-18." ;
    pm:searchedFor "A count rather than a place: every single prescription of the non-divinatory half, read at its own column and line, and for each of them whether the work names an announced sign by which the operator would know it had worked and whether it names any outcome under which he would conclude it had not. SPEC §15 requires the two to be counted separately and over the whole holding of a work, and prima-materia#747 asked for the figures for this work once the list node was broken up." ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole translation of the named columns was read continuously, not searched, and each prescription entered as its own node before the count was taken; the resulting enumeration is traditions/demotic-recipes.ttl with seventy-seven nodes. The clause of outcome was then counted mechanically over a whitespace-normalised copy of the translation with the inline line numbers stripped, because the line numbers stand inside the sentences and break every phrase search: then he dies 4, then she dies 1, then he is blinded 3, then he sleeps 1, then he is upset 1, then her body falls 1, then they are separated 1 (as the clause without agreeing again for ever), shall not be healed 1, then she has a passion 2, then she feels 1, then it brings you 2, then she comes at once 1, comes to pass instantly 1, then it makes dreams 1, then the bone comes up 1, then it stops 1, then he recovers 1, then it is cured 1, then she is cured 1, and it is safe 1, then it is troublesome 1, then it does its work 4 with it does its work also 1 and it does its work very much 1." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-09" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-09" ;
    skos:note "The two counts, which are the answer prima-materia#747 asked for and which are now over the whole work and not over its divinatory part alone. Announced signs of success: seven or eight, every one of them in the divinatory half and collected at pmc:PdmSignaAdventus; in the seventy-seven prescriptions outside it, none. Conditions of failure, meaning an outcome under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work: none, in either half. What the non-divinatory half has instead is thirty-two clauses of outcome, listed in the terms above, and they are outcomes and not signs: the claim and the thing to be observed are the same event, so there is nothing for the operator to look for on the way and nothing he could see that the clause excludes."@en ;
    skos:note "Three things the count did turn up, and they are the material the examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 is after. A graded threshold at pmc:PdmCataplexiaCapitisAsini, where four days of the same operation are to produce sleep and seven days death, which is the only place in the work where a quantity decides between two stated outcomes. A stopping rule at pmc:PdmMorsusCanisPrimus, where the wound is addressed daily until it is well, which is the only end set by a condition of the patient rather than by a count. And a question put at a term at pmc:PdmPodagraInterrogatio, already entered. Against them stand the four stages of pmc:PdmAquaMulierisPrima through pmc:PdmAquaMulierisQuarta, nineteen days of treatment with no criterion between any two stages."@en ;
    skos:note "Limits. One translation, one editor, one optical transcription, and the count of prescriptions depends on where a reader cuts: five places in the work give the same prescription twice and are entered once with both loci, and five more stand once with a named outcome and once with the formula that it does its work, entered once with both. A reader who counted the doublets separately would reach eighty-seven. The cut taken here is the prescription as the work distinguishes it, and the places where it does not distinguish are named at the nodes."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourteen entries of col. XXIV and col. verso XXX whose whole content is an ingredient and a back-reference were counted as prescriptions, because the back-reference does the work of the instruction and the manual means them to be used. Omitted with reason, and named here so that the omission is not silent: the eye-paint at col. verso XXI/4-6 and the star at col. verso XXVIII/1-2, both too damaged to carry a stated outcome, and the clause for making the skin disease troublesome at col. XIII/25, whose ingredient is lost."@en .

